INT. SAFE HOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

A narrow room. Blinds drawn. A table, a laptop, a half-eaten sandwich going stale. The refrigerator hums loud.

ODETTE MARSH, forties, grey at the temples, wears a hooded sweatshirt over a good blouse. A cut on her cheek, dried. She checks the window slats, then the door deadbolt, then the window slats again.

She sits. Types. Stops.

On the laptop, a voice memo icon. She clicks it.

ODETTE (V.O.)
(her own voice, recorded, steady)
If you're hearing this, I didn't make it to Lisbon. The files are in the drive under my mother's maiden name.

Odette winces at the sound of herself. Scrubs her face with both hands.

ODETTE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Don't trust anyone from the ministry. Not even Paulo.

She mutes it. Stares at the screen.

ODETTE
(to herself)
Paulo.

A car passes outside. She goes still. Headlights sweep the blinds, slats of light across her face. The car continues. She exhales.

ODETTE (CONT'D)
Okay. Okay.

She unmutes the memo.

ODETTE (V.O.)
There is a second drive. Under the sink. Behind the pipe.

She looks at the sink. At the cabinet below it. Rises. Opens it. Reaches behind the pipe.

Her hand closes on something. She pulls out a small black drive wrapped in plastic.

Outside, a second car door closes. Not passing. Parked.

She doesn't move. The refrigerator cuts off. The room is silent.

Footsteps on gravel. Slow. Two sets.

ODETTE (V.O.)
(from the laptop, urgent)
If you hear footsteps, don't run. Don't turn on any light.

Odette kills the laptop screen. Darkness. She backs against the wall, drive clutched against her chest.

The doorknob turns. Locked. A pause. The knob turns again.

She holds her breath.
